WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.632
When something breaks, the urge is to change three things, and hope.

2
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:08.367
Kubernetes punishes that.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:13.887
Many parts react on their own, so a guess can change the evidence before you have read it.

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.779
A fixed order of checks is faster, even when you think you already know the answer.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.595
Work from the bottom up, because each layer depends on the one below it.

6
00:00:23.595 --> 00:00:24.489
First the Pod.

7
00:00:24.489 --> 00:00:27.297
Is it scheduled, running, ready, restarting?

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.569
Then the Service.

9
00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:33.913
Does it select those Pods, and does its targetPort match the port the app listens on?

10
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:35.109
Then the route.

11
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:38.819
Does the Gateway or Ingress send traffic to that Service?

12
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:42.254
The node sits under every Pod, yet it comes last.

13
00:00:42.254 --> 00:00:48.433
A sick node shows up as many Pods failing at once, so a single broken Pod rarely points there.

14
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.085
For each layer, the commands are nearly always the same.

15
00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:56.085
kubectl get, for STATUS, READY and RESTARTS.

16
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.253
kubectl describe, reading the Events at the bottom first.

17
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:05.188
kubectl logs, with --previous if the container restarted.

18
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.610
Then up a layer, to the Service and its endpoints.

19
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.829
And the node, last.

20
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.468
Start with a Pod that never starts.

21
00:01:12.468 --> 00:01:14.207
kubectl get says Pending.

22
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:17.217
Pending means the scheduler found no node.

23
00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:21.657
The container has not run yet, so kubectl logs has nothing to show.

24
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:23.604
The answer is in the Events.

25
00:01:23.604 --> 00:01:28.259
Read the FailedScheduling event slowly, because it accounts for every node.

26
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.844
Zero of three nodes are available.

27
00:01:30.844 --> 00:01:34.341
One is ruled out by a taint: kind's control plane.

28
00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:36.999
And two lack free CPU for the request.

29
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.359
So the request is larger than any node's free room.

30
00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:43.891
Lower the request, remove other workloads, or add a node.

31
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.111
Next, the lab's catalog service.

32
00:01:47.111 --> 00:01:50.879
Its status is ErrImagePull, then ImagePullBackOff.

33
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.300
Again, the container never ran, so there are no logs.

34
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.609
Go to describe, and read the Events.

35
00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:02.843
BackOff in a status only means the kubelet now waits longer between tries.

36
00:02:02.843 --> 00:02:04.332
It is never the cause.

37
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:06.556
Look for the first error.

38
00:02:06.556 --> 00:02:10.520
Failed to pull image hello-devops, version 1.0.1.

39
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:13.855
But the image loaded into kind is 1.0.0.

40
00:02:13.855 --> 00:02:23.376
So check the name and the tag, the registry login for private images, and on kind, whether you ran kind load docker-image.

41
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.064
Now reports.

42
00:02:25.064 --> 00:02:29.525
RESTARTS keeps going up, and the status says CrashLoopBackOff.

43
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:35.009
The container keeps exiting, and the kubelet waits longer before each restart.

44
00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:38.323
10 seconds, then 20, then 40, up to five minutes.

45
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.458
CrashLoopBackOff is a symptom.

46
00:02:40.458 --> 00:02:43.923
But this time the container ran, so there is evidence.

47
00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:46.147
Read logs --previous.

48
00:02:46.147 --> 00:02:50.257
That shows the container that crashed, not the fresh one.

49
00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:51.566
Its log is empty.

50
00:02:51.566 --> 00:02:55.963
The process was killed from outside, before it could print anything.

51
00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:59.256
So read Last State in describe.

52
00:02:59.256 --> 00:03:01.049
Reason, OOMKilled.

53
00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:02.544
Exit code, 137.

54
00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:05.723
The container went over its memory limit.

55
00:03:05.723 --> 00:03:08.391
Its file sets the limit to only 40Mi.

56
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.717
The exit code narrows things down fast.

57
00:03:11.717 --> 00:03:14.170
Zero, the process finished on purpose.

58
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.687
One, the app exited with its own error.

59
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.151
137, killed with SIGKILL, most often the out of memory killer.

60
00:03:22.151 --> 00:03:26.385
143, stopped with SIGTERM, the polite stop signal.

61
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:27.892
Then search.

62
00:03:27.892 --> 00:03:30.005
Both Pods are Running and ready.

63
00:03:30.005 --> 00:03:32.645
Yet requests to the search Service fail.

64
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:38.399
Every Pod looks healthy, so move up a layer, to the Service, and ask for its endpoints.

65
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.046
Unset.

66
00:03:39.046 --> 00:03:43.285
The Service matches no Pods, so it sends traffic nowhere.

67
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.083
Compare the Service's selector with the Pods' labels.

68
00:03:47.083 --> 00:03:49.652
The selector asks for app: search-api.

69
00:03:49.652 --> 00:03:51.883
The Pods are labeled app: search.

70
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.267
They do not match.

71
00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:58.129
Fix the selector, and check that targetPort is the port the app listens on.

72
00:03:58.933 --> 00:03:59.817
And the node?

73
00:03:59.817 --> 00:04:03.625
Look there when many Pods fail at once, all on one node.

74
00:04:03.625 --> 00:04:07.977
kubectl get nodes shows NotReady when a kubelet stops reporting.

75
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:16.548
Then kubectl describe node shows conditions such as MemoryPressure and DiskPressure, and lists the Pods on it with their requests.

76
00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:19.020
One habit matters most.

77
00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.541
Deleting the broken Pod throws away the evidence.

78
00:04:22.541 --> 00:04:28.075
Events are kept for about an hour, so run describe and logs --previous first.

79
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.317
Debug from the bottom up: Pod, then Service, then route, then node.

80
00:04:33.317 --> 00:04:34.666
Get, describe, logs.

81
00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:36.756
And change one thing at a time.
